### Validation, queries and endpoint

The graph is validated with SHACL {{ cite("w3c_shacl") }} using pySHACL {{ cite("sw_pyshacl") }},
with SPARQL-based targets and constraints enabled and no inference. There are
{{ f.shacl.shapes }} node shapes for cardinalities, datatypes, identifier formats, evidence tiers
and provenance. SHACL-SPARQL constraints cover rules that a property path cannot express, such
as one current identification per organism, or a mapping record that must describe a SKOS
triple asserted in the graph. Only results of severity Violation stop the pipeline. A test
injects sixteen independent faults into a copy of the graph, covering every node shape, and
requires each to be reported on the right node.

There are {{ f.q.n_sparql | words }} SPARQL queries {{ cite("w3c_sparql") }} over the graph and
{{ f.q.n_sql | words }} SQL queries over the canonical tables in DuckDB {{ cite("raasveldt2019") }}.
Each SQL query must return exactly the rows of its SPARQL counterpart. The graph was also loaded
into an Oxigraph SPARQL endpoint {{ cite("sw_oxigraph") }} through the SPARQL 1.1 Graph Store
Protocol, and every query was run through the SPARQL 1.1 Protocol and compared with the
in-process results.

### Reproducibility

The pipeline is a Python {{ f.versions.python }} package with a locked environment. It runs as
seven stages (Fig. 1), each logging its record counts in and out. Continuous integration on
every pull request runs the linter and the test suite, then the pipeline offline from the cached
API responses, and fails if any regenerated output differs from the committed one. A Docker
build reruns the pipeline and the endpoint check.

The claim is determinism, not validity: the same PDF, cache and lockfile give byte-identical
outputs. The Turtle file contains no blank nodes, the JSON-LD is written in a sorted canonical
form, and the SHACL report is canonicalised. Continuous integration runs on one platform (Linux
x86-64). Re-running the resolution against the live services would give a different, newer
snapshot; that is a deliberate step that renews the cache.

Development used one feature branch and one reviewed pull request per stage. Claude Code
(Anthropic) was used as a coding and writing assistant; all curation decisions and results were
reviewed by the author.

## Results

### Extraction and harmonisation

All parts of the article listed in Table 1 were extracted. The row counts match the numbers the
article states: {{ s.t2 }} collected and {{ s.t3 }} examined specimens. The second, independent
transcription agreed with the extraction on all {{ f.census.table1.cells_agree }} occupied cells
and all {{ f.census.table1.codes_agree }} codes of Table 1, on all
{{ f.census.table2.fields_agree | thousands }} fields of Table 2 and all
{{ f.census.table3.fields_agree }} fields of Table 3: no disagreement. Harmonisation produced
the canonical tables in Table 2. No record was lost: every specimen became one occurrence, and
every Table 1 code became one assertion. The only exception is the {{ c.n_folded | words }} codes for
new records ("N"), which qualify a survey code and were merged into it. Counts that cannot be
rebuilt from the text alone are derived in the reconciliation table (Additional file 1).

