The {{ r.n_taxa }} names are the {{ f.names.table1 }} taxa of Table 1, one family-level
identification in Table 2, and {{ f.names.table3_previous | words }} previous identifications in Table 3. Of the
{{ f.loc.columns }} columns of Table 1, {{ f.loc.areas }} are areas; the last, "Open Sea
Distribution", is not a place, and its two assertions have no location. Of the
{{ f.loc.sites }} collecting sites, {{ f.loc.sites_linked }} were linked to a Table 1 area;
{{ f.loc.crosswalk_t3_rejected | words }} links the author judged uncertain were left unlinked, and one site has no
Table 1 column.

Working through the article cell by cell brought to light problems that a reader would hardly
notice:

- {{ c.typos | words }} misspelled names in Table 2;
- an impossible date in Table 3 ("31/04/2012");
- {{ s.t3_missing_state | words }} missing state and {{ d.by_id.W02 | words }} missing localities;
- {{ d.by_id.W03 | words }} museum specimens whose identification "could not be corroborated";
- the same order named Zoanthidea in Table 1 and Zoantharia in the species accounts;
- *Zoanthus pulchellus* in Table 1 but *Z.* cf. *pulchellus* in its species account and in
  Table 2.

Coordinates exist only for the {{ c.sites_with_coords }} collecting sites of Table 2, which fall in
{{ f.loc.areas_with_coords | words }} of the {{ f.loc.areas }} areas; no locality was geocoded. Collection dates are
stated in the Methods of the article for four of the sampled areas, so {{ c.t2_dated }} of the
{{ s.t2 }} collected specimens carry a date (November 2012). The others were left undated rather
than assumed. The literature assertions carry no date at all; their source's year of
publication is only an upper bound.

### Identifier resolution

WoRMS resolved all {{ r.worms.accepted }} names and GBIF {{ r.gbif.accepted }}. NCBI Taxonomy
resolved {{ r.ncbi.accepted - r.ncbi.via_accepted }} by exact name and
{{ r.ncbi.via_accepted | words }} more only through their WoRMS accepted name (`skos:closeMatch`) (Fig. 3,
Table 3). The three services are not independent: the GBIF Backbone draws on WoRMS for marine
groups, so agreement between them is not cross-validation. WoRMS held more than one record for
{{ r.homonyms_total | words }} of the names. For {{ r.homonyms_by_authority | words }} of them, the authority
printed in the article picked the right record. For example, *Zoanthus sociatus* (Ellis, 1768)
was matched to the accepted record, not to a *taxon inquirendum* of the same name by Le Sueur
(1818). The remaining name, *Ceriantheomorphe brasiliensis*, is printed without an authority, and
both of its records point to the same accepted name, which the author confirmed.

The fuzzy candidates show why fuzzy matches are never accepted automatically. GBIF matched the
historical label *Palythoa caribbeorum* to *Palythoa caribbea*, a different species, with a
score of 0.98. The author rejected that match and accepted WoRMS' phonetic match to
*P. caribaeorum*, as an incorrect subsequent spelling. The decision rests on Table 3 of the
article, which gives the two names as the previous and current identification of the same
specimen; it was not checked against the voucher. All {{ r.overrides | words }} curated decisions
({{ r.overrides_accept | words }} accepted, {{ r.overrides_reject | words }} rejected) are rows with
evidence, and the pipeline fails if any of them is never used.

