## Discussion

**Keeping the publication intact.** The central design choice was to treat the article as data
to be preserved, not as text to be corrected. Names are kept as name usages and linked to
current records with SKOS and SSSOM rather than replaced. The 2016 inventory and today's
taxonomy therefore coexist in one graph, each with its own provenance, and the drift between
them can be queried (Table 4). An `owl:sameAs` link would have merged a synonym with its
accepted name and lost exactly that distinction. The links stay at the level of names: when
providers disagree about circumscription, as NCBI and WoRMS do for *Bunodeopsis antilliensis*,
the graph shows the disagreement instead of hiding it. Keeping the specimen tables apart from
the distribution matrix is what made the cross-source checks possible.

**Judgement where it is needed, code everywhere else.** Most of the work is deterministic and
tested: extraction and its census, rule-based harmonisation, exact matching, validation. A
small number of decisions need expertise, such as whether a misspelled museum label denotes a
given species, whether two names differ only in gender agreement, or whether a collecting site
belongs to a locality of Table 1. Here all such decisions fill {{ c.curated_rows }} rows in
{{ c.curated_files | words }} curated files. Keeping them as data rather than code has three effects:

- each decision carries its evidence and the date it was reviewed;
- a decision that no longer applies is detected, because the pipeline fails on an unused
  override;
- the line between what the machine asserted and what a person asserted is explicit, encoded
  in the mapping justification (lexical matching or manual curation).

It also makes them auditable by someone other than the curator, which matters here because the
curator is a co-author of the source.

**Reading the cross-table differences.** The legend refers code "a" to Appendix 2 (Table 3), so
a museum code with no specimen there is a genuine difference between the article's own tables.
Of the {{ x.rows }} differences, {{ x.explained }} have an identifiable cause, mostly a coarse
identification or a taxon coded otherwise in the matrix; only
{{ (x.n_by_cause.reidentified + x.n_by_cause.unconfirmed) | words }} reflect identifications the article revised or
could not confirm. The rest are unreconciled between tables rather than
errors: the article does not say whether survey codes rest on specimens or on observations,
and the matrix may record identifications as they stood in the collection catalogue. The
classification is offered to the source authors as a list to check against their records.

**Fitness for use.** The graph is suitable for checklist work, for tracing each record to its
evidence, and for reconciling the 2016 names with current taxonomy. It is not, as it stands,
suitable for species distribution models or for area-of-occupancy estimates in red-list
assessments. No area is georeferenced, the {{ f.loc.areas }} areas range from a single beach to a
gulf, literature records have no date, and depth is not recorded. Presence is reported without
any record of absence or effort, beyond the flag on the six sampled localities. Within those
limits the evidence tiers let a reuser choose which records to rely on: for instance, the
{{ f.tiers.SurveyVouchered + f.tiers.MuseumTraced }} assertions backed by a specimen listed in the article.

**Limitations.** Seven limitations apply.

- **One article.** The parser relies on the layout of this article and refuses unexpected
  layouts rather than guessing. Only the layers after extraction (canonical model, resolution,
  data quality, graph) are expected to transfer; extraction must be rebuilt for each source.
- **Curation by a co-author.** The {{ c.curated_rows }} curated rows were made and reviewed by the
  same person, who co-authored the source article. They have not been audited independently.
- **Habitat per species.** Habitat is attached to species as the article reports it; half of the
  taxa have none, and ENVO lacks a class for coral rubble.
- **No georeferencing.** Areas are kept as named; a derived, separately versioned georeference
  layer would be needed for spatial reuse.
- **Heuristic scores.** The scores of fuzzy matches only ordered candidates for review and are
  not probabilities.
- **Placeholder namespaces.** The namespaces are not yet registered, so the graph's IRIs do not
  dereference.
- **A dated snapshot.** Resolution reflects the external services on the retrieval date.
  Refreshing is deliberate, by renewing the cache, and the changes appear as differences in the
  committed mappings.

**Next steps.** Several extensions follow directly from the model:

- An independent audit of the curated decisions by an anthozoan taxonomist, and a review of the
  unreconciled cross-table differences with the source authors.
- A derived georeference layer, with point-radius uncertainty, linked to each named area.
- Publication of the canonical tables to GBIF as a Darwin Core Archive with a dataset DOI.
- Later morphological data on the same specimens, such as cnidae measurements of *Condylactis
  gigantea*, added as Darwin Core measurements on the existing occurrences.
- Molecular data linked to the same specimens: tissue samples and sequences as material
  entities and DNA-derived data, joined through the catalogue numbers, which already serve as
  stable keys.
- Re-running the resolution periodically, which would turn Table 4 into a record of taxonomic
  change.

## Conclusions

A species inventory published as a PDF can be made machine-actionable without rewriting it.
In this case:

- every one of the {{ s.t1_codes }} distribution codes and {{ s.t2 + s.t3 }} specimen records
  is traceable to its page, table and row, and a second, independent transcription agrees with
  every one of them;
- every identifier link records how, when and by whom it was made;
- the resulting graph has no SHACL violation, is queryable with SPARQL, and is rebuilt byte for
  byte from its committed inputs.

The process showed that {{ q03.valid_changed | words }} names the article uses as valid now have a different
accepted name, that its three tables disagree in {{ x.rows - x.explained }} places that no
nomenclatural or coding explanation resolves, and that {{ cl.pct_lit_only }}% of its taxon × area
cells rest on literature alone, most of them on a single monograph. The PDF shows none of
this, and all of it matters to anyone reusing the inventory.

